93:#!/usr/bin/env python3
94:"""py2mpy — a *pure* CPython-AST -> K-constructor transliterator.
95:
96:Reads a .py file and prints the matching `.mpy` term: a Lisp-indented tree of K
97:constructors that a (future) K semantics will parse. The design rule is strict:
98:
99:    PURE TRANSLITERATION. Every Python AST node maps to exactly one K
100:    constructor with the same structure. The front-end makes NO semantic
101:    decisions — it does not desugar, simplify, normalize, or "interpret"
102:    anything. All meaning lives in the K semantics that consumes the term.
103:
104:Consequences of that rule (vs. a "helpful" front-end):
105:
106:  * `is` / `is not` transliterate to `CmpOp("is", ...)` / `CmpOp("is not", ...)`
107:    — NOT to `==` / `!=`. K decides what identity means.
108:  * a chained comparison `a < b < c` stays a single `Compare` carrying the
109:    chain (`Compare(a, CmpOp("<", b), CmpOp("<", c))`) — it is NOT pre-expanded
110:    into `a < b and b < c`.
111:  * a method call `x.append(v)` stays `Call(Attribute(Name("x"), "append"), v)`
112:    — there is no special `MethodCall` constructor folding the receiver in.
113:  * `a[i] = v` stays `Assign(Subscript(Name("a"), Name("i")), v)` — the target
114:    is just an expression in store position; no special `SubscriptAssign`.
115:
116:The only choices the transliterator makes are unavoidably *syntactic* — e.g. a
117:`bool` literal becomes `Bool(...)` not `Int(...)`, because that is the literal's
118:actual Python type as read off the AST, not a semantic judgement.
119:
120:Anything outside the covered subset raises `Unsupported`, naming the node, so
121:coverage gaps fail loudly instead of being silently dropped.
122:
123:Usage:
124:    python scripts/py2mpy.py FILE.py          # print the .mpy term (default)
125:    python scripts/py2mpy.py FILE.py --ast    # print the Python AST (ast.dump)
126:"""
127:from __future__ import annotations
